Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               python3
Standards-Version: 4.7.2
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: apt-emerge
Architecture: all
Depends: python3 (>= 3.9),
         ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: apt
Suggests: gpgv,
          dpkg-dev
Description: Portage-flavoured package manager front-end for Debian
 emerge speaks Gentoo Portage's command line and prints Portage-style output,
 but everything underneath is real Debian tooling -- apt, dpkg and their
 indexes. Nothing is emulated and it invents no state of its own: @selected
 really is apt's set of manually-installed packages.
 .
 Beyond translating the familiar verbs (-av, -uD @world, -C, --depclean, -s,
 --sync, -b) it adds three things Debian does not have:
 .
  * --no-dep-upgrade installs a package without upgrading anything already
    on the system. It is not a pin: it searches, taking the newest version of
    each not-yet-installed dependency whose whole subtree leaves installed
    packages alone. When nothing fits it names the package that formed the
    wall, and --with permits that one to move. Useful for installing a leaf
    without dragging up libc, the kernel, or your graphics stack.
  * --dispatch-conf reviews the config files dpkg parks as .dpkg-dist. It
    archives each package's as-shipped config at install time and uses it as
    the common ancestor for a real 3-way merge of your edits against the new
    version, so only genuine conflicts are put to you. Also reachable as
    dispatch-conf and etc-update.
  * It warns when an upgrade touches the running graphical session, derived
    from the live session rather than a hardcoded list, and distinguishes a
    same-upstream rebuild (your session keeps running) from a real version
    change (it may not).
 .
 Two backends are selected automatically: apt where apt-get is present, and a
 self-contained dpkg-only backend -- with its own sources parsing, index sync,
 gpgv verification, resolver and world file -- for embedded systems that carry
 only dpkg.
 .
 It installs as a single stdlib-only Python file with no dependencies beyond
 python3 itself, so a box it is installed on can be repaired with a text
 editor.
